# One React component per file

Each `.tsx` file exports exactly one React component. The file name matches
the component name in `PascalCase` (e.g. `CharacterCard.tsx` exports
`CharacterCard`).

If you need a helper component, move it to its own file and import it.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD: two components in one file
// CharacterCard.tsx
export function Avatar({ src }: { src: string }) {
  return <img src={src} />
}

export function CharacterCard({ character }: { character: Character }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <Avatar src={character.avatarUrl} />
      <span>{character.name}</span>
    </div>
  )
}
```

```tsx
// ✅ GOOD: split into Avatar.tsx and CharacterCard.tsx
// Avatar.tsx
export function Avatar({ src }: { src: string }) {
  return <img src={src} />
}

// CharacterCard.tsx
import { Avatar } from './Avatar'

export function CharacterCard({ character }: { character: Character }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <Avatar src={character.avatarUrl} />
      <span>{character.name}</span>
    </div>
  )
}
```

## Allowed exceptions

- **Hooks and utilities** colocated with their owning component are fine
  (e.g. `useCharacterCard` in `CharacterCard.tsx`).
- **Types and constants** used only by the component may live in the
  same file.
- **Trivial render-only subcomponents** kept private (not exported) are
  acceptable only when they are tiny and would never be reused. When in
  doubt, extract.
